Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT vs TOML — Flat configuration vs hierarchical documents
	Metadata:
		Author: Claude Fable 5
		Revision: Joan Costa Mombiela
		Last modif: 2026-09-08
		Description: STXT and TOML compared with examples: headers versus indentation, nested tables, multi-line strings, types in the syntax versus types in the definition, and validation.

	Header: @STXT@ vs TOML

	Assert >>
		TOML and @STXT@ share a goal: files a person writes and reads.\\
		They differ in the model: tables of typed key-value pairs versus a tree of text nodes.

	Content >>
		TOML is the configuration format of `Cargo.toml` and `pyproject.toml`, and its
		specification states a goal close to that of @STXT@: a minimal format, easy to
		read due to obvious semantics. The difference lies in the data model: TOML
		describes tables of keys with typed values; @STXT@, hierarchical documents whose
		values are text.

	Subheader: The same content, twice

	Content >>
		A backup configuration in TOML:

	Listing >>
		title = "Nightly backup"

		[storage]
		kind = "s3"
		bucket = "acme-backups"

		[[job]]
		name = "database"
		schedule = "02:30"
		keep = 14

		[[job]]
		name = "documents"
		schedule = "03:00"
		keep = 30

	Content >>
		And in @STXT@:

	Code >>
		Backup: Nightly backup
			Storage:
				Kind: s3
				Bucket: acme-backups
			Job: database
				Schedule: 02:30
				Keep: 14
			Job: documents
				Schedule: 03:00
				Keep: 30

	Content >>
		The tree is the same. What changes is where the hierarchy lives: in TOML, in the
		`[storage]` and `[[job]]` headers; in @STXT@, in the indentation.

	Subheader: Where the hierarchy lives

	Content >>
		Every TOML header writes the full path of its table, and an element of a nested
		array of tables repeats it whole. Two jobs with their steps:

	Listing >>
		[[job]]
		name = "database"

		[[job.step]]
		run = "pg_dump acme > acme.sql"

		[[job.step]]
		run = "gzip acme.sql"

		[[job]]
		name = "documents"

		[[job.step]]
		run = "tar cf documents.tar /srv/docs"

	Content >>
		Each `[[job.step]]` belongs to the last `[[job]]` opened: membership is decided by
		position in the file, and the path is repeated in every header. In @STXT@ every
		level is written once and a child belongs to the node it is indented under
		([STXT-SPEC §8](stxt-core-ref#s8)):

	Code >>
		Backup:
			Job: database
				Step: pg_dump acme > acme.sql
				Step: gzip acme.sql
			Job: documents
				Step: tar cf documents.tar /srv/docs

	Content >>
		Two more properties of the TOML model: tables can be written in any order, so the
		visual shape of the file need not match the tree; and the same structure admits
		several spellings —headers, dotted keys (`storage.kind = "s3"`), inline tables
		(`storage = { kind = "s3" }`), inline arrays and arrays of tables—. In @STXT@ the
		visual shape of the document is its structure, and there is a single spelling.

	Subheader: Multi-line text

	Content >>
		A TOML multi-line string keeps everything between the delimiters except the
		initial line break, including the indentation of each line. For the value not to
		contain it, the text cannot be indented along with the table it belongs to:

	Listing >>
		[storage]
		kind = "s3"
		notes = """
		Restarted after the 2026-08-31 incident.
		The TLS certificate expires on 2027-03-01.
		"""

	Content >>
		In an @STXT@ `>>` block the indentation of the level is discounted and only the
		additional indentation is part of the text: the block is indented with the
		document and arrives literal, with no escapes ([STXT-SPEC §10](stxt-core-ref#s10)).

	Code >>
		Storage:
			Kind: s3
			Notes >>
				Restarted after the 2026-08-31 incident.
				The TLS certificate expires on 2027-03-01.

	Subheader: Types

	Content >>
		In TOML the type is in the spelling of the value: `14` is an integer, `"14"` a
		string, `true` a boolean, `2026-08-31T02:30:00Z` a date-time. A TOML parser
		returns typed data with no external definition; in return, strings always go in
		quotes, in four forms —basic and literal, single-line and multi-line—, with
		escapes in the basic ones.

		In @STXT@ a value is always text, without quotes. Types are declared in the
		definition —`NATURAL`, `DATE`, `TIMESTAMP`, `EMAIL`…— and checked by the
		validator, not the parser: without a definition, `14` is two characters.

	Subheader: Namespaces and validation

	Content >>
		TOML has no namespaces and no schema layer: nothing in the file declares which
		vocabulary it belongs to, and validation is left to the application or an
		external tool. In @STXT@ the namespace connects the document with its definition,
		and the template fixes structure, cardinalities and types. The initial
		configuration, with a namespace:

	Code >>
		Backup (com.example.backup): Nightly backup
			Storage:
				Kind: s3
				Bucket: acme-backups
			Job: database
				Schedule: 02:30
				Keep: 14
			Job: documents
				Schedule: 03:00
				Keep: 30

	Content >>
		And the template for its namespace:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.backup
			Structure >>
				Backup (com.example.backup):
					Storage: (1)
						Kind: (1)
						Bucket: (1)
					Job: (+)
						Schedule: (1)
						Keep: (1) NATURAL

	Content >>
		An undeclared name, a missing `Storage` or a `Keep` that is not a number fail
		with an explicit error code:

	Code >>
		# ERROR: Keep requires a NATURAL value
		Backup (com.example.backup): Nightly backup
			Storage:
				Kind: s3
				Bucket: acme-backups
			Job: database
				Schedule: 02:30
				Keep: fourteen

	Subheader: When to use each

	Content >>
		TOML, where the ecosystem expects it —`Cargo.toml`, `pyproject.toml`, the
		configuration of tools that read that file— and for flat configuration of keys
		with typed values, which is the goal its specification states.

		@STXT@, when the file grows into a document: several levels of hierarchy, prose
		next to the data, [corporate records](use-cases-corporate-docs) and any structure
		that must be validated against a definition. The
		[configuration files](use-cases-config-files) use case develops the closest
		scenario, and the short version of this comparison is in the
		[FAQ](faq#why-not-xml-yaml-or-toml).
